## Decisions locked this session

- **Runs 1–4 measured NOTHING.** The calibration harness never reset ledger state between runs, so
  each run inherited the previous run's verdicts. Do NOT reason from any run 1–4 number.
- **A false close was terminal**: `ObligationResurfacingJudge` queries only `Open|Advanced`
  (~line 127), so a wrongly-Closed row was never revisited. Fixed by the reset; the underlying
  product risk (one bad auto-close silently drops a real obligation forever) is noted, not fixed.
- **Grounding ≠ relevance.** `QuoteGrounding.Contains` proves a quote is real text, never that it
  answers the debt. Run 3 closed "the stopped clock at a quarter past three" with "…smoked a cigar
  and waited behind a tree…". Now vetoed by `ObligationResurfacingJudge.SharesContent`.
- **Diagnosis corrected twice.** Over-extraction is NOT the dominant term. Run 5 close-rate by
  chapter — ch1 **82%**, later chapters 15–59%, across twelve structurally identical self-contained
  Holmes stories — shows the closer degrades as the open pile grows. The starved rows are real debts
  the story does pay (masked visitor = King of Bohemia; Spaulding's cellar = the tunnel; typewriter
  wear convicts Hosmer Angel; the locked attic room). The extractor was right; the model was never
  shown the row it was paying.
- **The author redeploys the Hub, never me.** Calibration executes inside
  `C:\Apps\MindAttic\Prose\Hub.exe`; `Prose.Cli` is only a forwarder to `127.0.0.1:5900`. A local
  build changes nothing at runtime.
- **One variable per run**, so results stay attributable.
- Verse-index recommendation given (see Open questions): build verses as a *derived retrieval
  index*; do NOT store verse indices as obligation pointers.

